1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,680
On a hot day when you haven't had

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:04,000
much to drink, you pass a small amount

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:05,800
of dark yellow urine.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,400
Drink a litre of water quickly, and within

5
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,240
an hour or so you pass a lot

6
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,840
of pale urine.

7
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:15,280
Same kidneys, same day, opposite results.

8
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:18,480
The colour is a clue: similar wastes dissolved

9
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,880
in less water, or in more.

10
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:22,960
So what tells your kidneys how much water

11
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:24,880
to keep, and how do they do it?

12
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,900
The variable here isn't how much you drink.

13
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:31,860
It's how concentrated your blood is: how much

14
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:34,580
dissolved salt and other particles there are in

15
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:36,660
each litre of blood plasma.

16
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:39,540
Biologists call this osmolarity.

17
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:42,180
Lose water by sweating or breathing out, and

18
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:45,220
the same particles sit in less water, so

19
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:47,140
osmolarity rises.

20
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:50,473
Drink lots of water, and osmolarity falls.

21
00:00:50,607 --> 00:00:53,700
Remember osmosis from Year 11: water moves across

22
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,340
a membrane towards the side with more dissolved

23
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:57,460
particles.

24
00:00:57,540 --> 00:00:59,700
That rule explains most of this video.

25
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,700
Your kidneys control how much water leaves.

26
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,820
Each kidney holds about a million tiny tubes

27
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:08,220
called nephrons.

28
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,340
At the start of each one, blood is

29
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:13,300
filtered, and water and small particles are pushed

30
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:14,580
into the tube.

31
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,540
This fluid, the filtrate, then flows along the

32
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:19,780
tubule, and most of it is taken back

33
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:20,900
into the blood.

34
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,740
That's reabsorption.

35
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:26,260
Filtering isn't choosy about water, so control comes

36
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:28,020
from what's taken back.

37
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:31,060
Here's the surprise: your kidneys filter around a

38
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:33,540
hundred and eighty litres a day, yet you

39
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:36,100
pass only about one and a half.

40
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,980
The last stretch, the collecting duct, sets how

41
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:40,340
much of the rest you keep.

42
00:01:41,567 --> 00:01:43,286
So who's measuring?

43
00:01:43,567 --> 00:01:47,406
In the hypothalamus are osmoreceptors, cells that sense

44
00:01:47,487 --> 00:01:48,926
blood concentration.

45
00:01:49,247 --> 00:01:52,286
When blood gets too concentrated, water leaves them

46
00:01:52,367 --> 00:01:54,926
by osmosis and they shrink slightly.

47
00:01:55,007 --> 00:01:56,606
That's the detection.

48
00:01:56,647 --> 00:01:59,406
The hypothalamus then sends nerve impulses down to

49
00:01:59,487 --> 00:02:02,153
the posterior pituitary, the back part of a

50
00:02:02,207 --> 00:02:04,286
small gland just below it.

51
00:02:04,327 --> 00:02:08,766
The pituitary releases ADH, antidiuretic hormone, into the

52
00:02:08,927 --> 00:02:09,806
blood.

53
00:02:09,887 --> 00:02:12,606
ADH is made in the hypothalamus, but it's

54
00:02:12,807 --> 00:02:15,726
stored in and released from the pituitary.

55
00:02:15,807 --> 00:02:17,966
So one step is neural, and the next

56
00:02:18,047 --> 00:02:18,846
is hormonal.

57
00:02:19,567 --> 00:02:22,446
The hypothalamus also makes you thirsty, because your

58
00:02:22,527 --> 00:02:25,806
kidneys can save water, but only drinking replaces

59
00:02:25,887 --> 00:02:26,206
it.

60
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:29,833
ADH travels in the blood to the collecting

61
00:02:29,914 --> 00:02:30,393
ducts.

62
00:02:30,874 --> 00:02:33,353
There, it binds to the duct cells and

63
00:02:33,434 --> 00:02:37,273
moves water channels, called aquaporins, from inside the

64
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:40,273
cells into the membrane facing the filtrate.

65
00:02:40,634 --> 00:02:43,753
The tissue around the ducts is very concentrated,

66
00:02:43,834 --> 00:02:46,233
so once the channels are in, water moves

67
00:02:46,314 --> 00:02:48,553
out of the duct by osmosis and back

68
00:02:48,674 --> 00:02:49,673
into the blood.

69
00:02:50,074 --> 00:02:52,073
Less water is left in the duct, so

70
00:02:52,154 --> 00:02:54,873
you make a smaller volume of more concentrated

71
00:02:54,954 --> 00:02:55,513
urine.

72
00:02:56,734 --> 00:02:59,853
As water returns to the blood, osmolarity falls

73
00:02:59,934 --> 00:03:01,533
back towards normal.

74
00:03:01,574 --> 00:03:05,133
The osmoreceptors swell back, their signals weaken, and

75
00:03:05,294 --> 00:03:07,373
less ADH is released.

76
00:03:07,494 --> 00:03:11,213
That's negative feedback: a rise in concentration led

77
00:03:11,254 --> 00:03:13,613
to a response that lowers it.

78
00:03:13,694 --> 00:03:16,893
ADH release varies with concentration and can become

79
00:03:17,094 --> 00:03:19,933
extremely low after a large water intake.

80
00:03:21,134 --> 00:03:22,733
Now the litre of water.

81
00:03:22,974 --> 00:03:26,173
It's absorbed into the blood, and osmolarity falls.

82
00:03:26,734 --> 00:03:31,053
Osmoreceptors detect the dilution, and ADH release drops.

83
00:03:31,374 --> 00:03:33,533
Aquaporins are taken back out of the duct

84
00:03:33,614 --> 00:03:36,813
membrane, so less water can leave the duct.

85
00:03:36,894 --> 00:03:39,133
More water stays in the filtrate, and you

86
00:03:39,214 --> 00:03:42,173
make a large volume of pale, dilute urine

87
00:03:42,254 --> 00:03:44,733
until concentration is back in range.

88
00:03:44,814 --> 00:03:46,813
Same loop, opposite direction.

89
00:03:48,034 --> 00:03:51,513
One more hormone often turns up beside ADH.

90
00:03:51,794 --> 00:03:54,513
Aldosterone, from the adrenal glands on top of

91
00:03:54,594 --> 00:03:57,633
the kidneys, responds mainly to low blood volume

92
00:03:57,714 --> 00:04:00,353
or pressure, such as after heavy fluid loss.

93
00:04:00,994 --> 00:04:02,753
It makes the later parts of the nephron

94
00:04:02,914 --> 00:04:04,513
reabsorb more sodium.

95
00:04:05,154 --> 00:04:07,873
When water channels are present, water follows the

96
00:04:07,954 --> 00:04:11,153
sodium by osmosis, helping restore blood volume.

97
00:04:11,874 --> 00:04:14,353
Aldosterone doesn't move water directly.

98
00:04:14,914 --> 00:04:17,473
ADH changes how much water can leave the

99
00:04:17,554 --> 00:04:20,593
duct, and aldosterone changes how much salt is

100
00:04:20,674 --> 00:04:21,553
taken back.

101
00:04:22,767 --> 00:04:26,446
To keep them apart: ADH is the anti-wee

102
00:04:26,487 --> 00:04:27,006
hormone.

103
00:04:27,487 --> 00:04:31,806
Diuresis means making urine, so antidiuretic means less

104
00:04:31,847 --> 00:04:32,206
of it.

105
00:04:32,687 --> 00:04:35,646
And for aldosterone, water follows salt.

106
00:04:36,867 --> 00:04:38,546
Two exam questions.

107
00:04:38,867 --> 00:04:39,346
First.

108
00:04:39,907 --> 00:04:42,146
A student eats a large packet of salted

109
00:04:42,227 --> 00:04:44,546
chips and drinks nothing for the next two

110
00:04:44,627 --> 00:04:45,186
hours.

111
00:04:45,747 --> 00:04:47,746
The salt is absorbed into the blood.

112
00:04:48,307 --> 00:04:51,186
Predict how ADH release and the student's urine

113
00:04:51,267 --> 00:04:52,306
will change.

114
00:04:52,627 --> 00:04:55,906
Then explain how this response limits further water

115
00:04:55,987 --> 00:04:58,786
loss, and why drinking water would help restore

116
00:04:58,867 --> 00:05:00,266
blood osmolarity.

117
00:05:00,627 --> 00:05:02,066
Pause here to answer.

118
00:05:12,467 --> 00:05:16,146
The absorbed salt raises blood osmolarity, even without

119
00:05:16,227 --> 00:05:16,706
sweating.

120
00:05:17,187 --> 00:05:21,026
Osmoreceptors detect it, and the posterior pituitary releases

121
00:05:21,107 --> 00:05:22,426
more ADH.

122
00:05:22,707 --> 00:05:25,826
More aquaporins let the collecting ducts reabsorb more

123
00:05:25,907 --> 00:05:28,786
water, so you make less urine, and it's

124
00:05:28,907 --> 00:05:29,906
more concentrated.

125
00:05:30,387 --> 00:05:32,386
That limits further water loss.

126
00:05:32,707 --> 00:05:35,506
Drinking supplies new water to dilute the salt.

127
00:05:36,067 --> 00:05:39,186
The classic mistake is expecting ADH to make

128
00:05:39,267 --> 00:05:40,066
new water.

129
00:05:41,267 --> 00:05:41,746
Second.

130
00:05:42,307 --> 00:05:45,106
On separate days, a healthy adult drank one

131
00:05:45,187 --> 00:05:48,146
litre of pure water, or one litre of

132
00:05:48,227 --> 00:05:50,706
a salt solution with the same concentration as

133
00:05:50,787 --> 00:05:51,826
their blood.

134
00:05:51,867 --> 00:05:54,146
The graph shows their urine output over three

135
00:05:54,427 --> 00:05:55,266
hours.

136
00:05:55,347 --> 00:05:58,226
Explain the urine output after drinking water, and

137
00:05:58,467 --> 00:06:01,746
explain why the salt solution caused little change.

138
00:06:01,907 --> 00:06:03,106
Pause here to answer.

139
00:06:13,534 --> 00:06:17,133
Pure water diluted the blood, so ADH release

140
00:06:17,214 --> 00:06:17,693
dropped.

141
00:06:18,174 --> 00:06:21,853
With fewer aquaporins, less water was reabsorbed, so

142
00:06:22,014 --> 00:06:24,573
urine output peaked at about an hour, then

143
00:06:24,654 --> 00:06:26,493
fell as concentration recovered.

144
00:06:27,134 --> 00:06:30,973
The salt solution matched blood concentration, so osmolarity

145
00:06:31,134 --> 00:06:34,093
barely changed and ADH stayed about the same.

146
00:06:34,814 --> 00:06:37,453
The classic mistake is saying the kidneys respond

147
00:06:37,534 --> 00:06:40,253
to how much you drink: both drinks were

148
00:06:40,334 --> 00:06:41,133
one litre.

149
00:06:42,334 --> 00:06:45,453
So for any water balance question, ask what

150
00:06:45,534 --> 00:06:49,293
happened to blood concentration, which way ADH moved,

151
00:06:49,374 --> 00:06:51,373
and what that did to the collecting ducts

152
00:06:51,454 --> 00:06:52,533
and the urine.

153
00:06:52,974 --> 00:06:56,653
Next, plants face the same problem without kidneys:

154
00:06:56,734 --> 00:06:58,413
how do they keep enough water?

155
00:06:59,634 --> 00:07:01,073
Here are the key notes.

156
00:07:01,314 --> 00:07:03,233
Pause the video if you need more time

157
00:07:03,314 --> 00:07:04,113
to copy them.
